{
  "findings": [
    {
      "severity": "note",
      "claim": "The claim sentence enumerating the witness's seven refused edits is dense enough to misread as describing eight cases (\"at the end of a file, and before a blank line and a line of lesser indent\" first scans as three separate cases, plus three \"Others\" cases, plus the final \"One adds nothing, and one adds a comment\" pair, totaling eight). It only resolves to the stated seven once \"before a blank line and a line of lesser indent\" is read as a single combined scenario (blank line skipped, then a lesser-indented kept line reached). The arithmetic is internally consistent once parsed correctly — the sixteen-None and twenty-two-wrong-version counts both check out exactly — so this is not a defect in the rule itself, only in how easily the prose is miscounted on a first read.",
      "criterion": 1,
      "file": null,
      "line": null,
      "fixes": null
    },
    {
      "severity": "note",
      "claim": "The claim states the added text is `replace` with its `find` \"taken out once,\" but never states that `find` occurs exactly once inside `replace`, only that `replace` \"holds its `find` whole\" (i.e., contains it). If `find` occurred more than once inside `replace`, which occurrence to split on is unstated. None of the seven refused, sixteen None, or wrong-version cases exercises this edge, so it costs nothing concretely against the witness, but the claim's own wording leaves it ambiguous.",
      "criterion": 1,
      "file": null,
      "line": null,
      "fixes": null
    },
    {
      "severity": "note",
      "claim": "The claim never states what happens when the find sits at the end of the file, so there is no following kept line at all, even though one of the seven named witness cases is exactly the SA-0390 edit refused \"at the end of a file.\" A builder has to infer that \"followed by a first non-blank kept line of before\" is vacuously satisfied when no such line exists. Because this exact scenario is one of the seven witness-driven cases, a build that mishandles it fails the witness directly, so the gap is self-correcting rather than a live risk, but the prose itself is terse on this point.",
      "criterion": 1,
      "file": null,
      "line": null,
      "fixes": null
    }
  ]
}
